  "items": [
    {
      "src": "II:4",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "We’ve seen that a mixed number can be written as a sum of fractions. For example, here’s a mixed number 2 3/5. It means two whole units, add three fifths. 2 + 3/5 And then the two can be written as fraction over one.",
      "expected": "2/1 + 3/5",
      "demo": "*2/1 + 3/5*"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:5",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "But these two fractions have different numbers on the bottom, and so the size of their parts is different.",
      "expected": "different",
      "demo": "The size of their parts is *different*.",
      "figures": [
        { "kind": "bar", "units": 2, "parts": 1, "counted": 2 },
        { "kind": "bar", "units": 1, "parts": 5, "counted": 3 }
      ]
    },
    {
      "src": "II:10",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "To write it as a sum of fractions with the same numbers on the bottom, we can first write it like this instead: 2 + 3/5 Then two is one plus one:",
      "expected": "1 + 1 + 3/5",
      "demo": "*1 + 1 + 3/5*"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:12",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "And with a five on the bottom, one can be written like this:",
      "expected": "5/5 + 5/5 + 3/5",
      "demo": "*5/5 + 5/5 + 3/5* Now all the fractions have the same number on the bottom: 2 3/5 = 5/5 + 5/5 + 3/5"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:18",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "To rewrite this mixed number as a sum of fractions, with the same number on the bottom: 2 3/7 First I write in the secret plus symbol:",
      "expected": "2 + 3/7",
      "demo": "*2 + 3/7*"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:20",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "Then write two as a sum of ones:",
      "expected": "1 + 1 + 3/7",
      "demo": "*1 + 1 + 3/7*"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:22",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
      "prompt": "Then rewrite the ones as fractions:",
      "expected": "7/7 + 7/7 + 3/7",
      "demo": "*7/7 + 7/7 + 3/7*"
    },
    {
      "src": "II:26",
      "role": "model",
      "mode": "typed",
